 "basis": {
  "what_this_file_is": "The cost of one end-to-end origin collection, recorded because the assessment rows cannot carry it. Everything here is wall clock, pacing, robots evidence and process memory; every byte count and every disposition lives in data/origins/2026-09-13-chihealth-com.assessments.jsonl and is deliberately not repeated here, so that no number in this project has two sources that can disagree.",
  "wall_clock": "time.monotonic() around the whole loop and around each assess_hospital_url call, in one process. `seconds` therefore includes robots.txt consultation, pacing, the HTTP request, the streamed download, gzip decoding, the full inspection, and the durable append.",
  "peak_rss_bytes": "resource.getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss, read after each target and at the end. It is a high-water mark for the whole process, so `peak_rss_bytes_after` is monotonic by construction and is not a per-target measurement.",
  "pacing": "The Politeness object's own evidence. One Politeness was shared by all 17 retrievals, so the per-host minimum interval held across the run rather than resetting per invocation.",
  "probe_pass": "A `mrf-honest probe` of each distinct URL before any body was retrieved: one ranged GET asking for the first 4,096 bytes with Accept-Encoding: identity, robots.txt first. It was run to price the order before downloading it. Its wire cost is NOT recorded because it is not measurable from here: every one of the 15 responses was HTTP 200 rather than 206, so the server ignored the Range header and began sending a full body that the client read 4 KiB of and closed. What arrived before the close is whatever was already in flight.",
  "not_measured": [
   "request and response header bytes, TLS handshake bytes, and TCP overhead: `wire_size_bytes` in the assessment rows counts response body bytes only",
   "the bandwidth the probe pass cost, for the reason stated above",
   "anything about a GitHub-hosted runner: this run was on the operator's laptop"
  ]
 },
